  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nThe problem requires finding a step function h on [0, 2] that minimizes the maximum overlap integral C\u2085. This task is inherently non-convex due to the bilinear terms in the overlap sums. Previous approaches using PSO, ES, and DE have shown promise but may struggle with local optima and convergence speed. \n\nThis strategy introduces a convex optimization-based approach with a novel repair mechanism to maintain feasibility. It leverages the disciplined convex programming framework to handle constraints and employs a binary search strategy to find the optimal value of C\u2085. The approach balances global exploration with local refinement while ensuring the solution adheres to the problem constraints.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\nVariables:\n- h: n_points-dimensional vector, h_i \u2208 [0,1], sum(h) = n_points / 2\n- DX: 2.0 / n_points (constant)\n- \u03bc: Minimum overlap target to be maximized\n\nObjective function:\nThe goal is to maximize \u03bc such that \u222bh(x)h(x+k)dx \u2265 \u03bc for all |k| < 2.\n\nConstraints:\n- 0 \u2264 h_i \u2264 1 for all i\n- sum(h_i) = n_points / 2\n- For sampled shifts k_j, \u222bh(x)h(x+k_j)dx \u2265 \u03bc\n\n3. Implementation procedure\na) Initialization:\n1. If `initial_h_values` exists, use it as a starting point and initialize \u03bc.\n2. Else, initialize h randomly with sum(h) = n_points / 2.\n\nb) Binary search on \u03bc:\n1. Set low = 0, high = 0.5, and perform a binary search to maximize \u03bc.\n2. For each candidate \u03bc, solve the feasibility problem.\n\nc) Feasibility check for \u03bc:\n1. Sample a grid of shifts k over [-2, 2].\n2. For each k_j, compute the overlap constraint \u222bh(x)h(x+k_j)dx \u2265 \u03bc.\n3. Use CVXOPT to solve the convex optimization problem.\n\nd) Repair mechanism:\n1. After each optimization step, clip h to [0,1].\n2. Adjust sum(h) to n_points / 2 by proportional scaling.\n\ne) Final refinement:\n1. Use gradient descent to further minimize C\u2085.\n2. Apply the repair mechanism after each step.\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations\n- n_points: 100\n- Sampling frequency for k: 50 points in [-2,2]\n- Binary search tolerance: 1e-5\n- Gradient descent learning rate: 0.001\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\n- Binary search steps: 20 iterations\n- CVXOPT solve per iteration: ~5s\n- Gradient descent: 100 steps\n- Total compute time: ~600s\n\n6. Validation and return contract\n- Ensure h_i \u2208 [0,1] and sum(h) = n_points / 2\n- Verify C\u2085 computation matches evaluate_erdos_solution()\n- Check feasibility of \u03bc after each optimization step",
